Agent: Title: Molecular Subtypes of Endometrial Cancer: Integrating Multi‑omic Discovery with Functional Validation to Improve Prognosis and Therapy Selection

Overall goal and central hypothesis
Endometrial cancer (EC) exhibits marked clinical heterogeneity poorly captured by histology and stage alone. We hypothesize that comprehensive multi‑omic characterization will (1) refine molecular subtypes beyond existing TCGA categories, (2) link subtypes to distinct disease biology and treatment sensitivities, and (3) yield robust biomarkers suitable for clinical risk stratification and therapeutic guidance.

Specific Aims
Aim 1 — Define and validate integrative molecular subtypes of endometrial cancer using multi‑omic profiling.
Hypothesis: Unsupervised integration of WES/WGS, RNA‑seq, DNA methylation, and proteomics across a large, clinically annotated EC cohort will identify reproducible subtypes with distinct mutational, pathway, and immune landscapes.
Approach: Assemble discovery cohort (n≈300 primary ECs; diverse histologies and stages) with matched clinical data. Perform whole‑exome sequencing, RNA‑seq, methylation array (or bisulfite-seq on subset), and mass‑spec proteomics. Use state‑of‑the‑art integration (similarity network fusion/ iClusterPlus / MOFA2) to derive subtypes; evaluate robustness by cross‑validation and internal bootstrapping. Validate subtype signatures in two independent cohorts (TCGA EC and an external cohort, combined n≥400) using classifiers (signatures for RNA/protein/IHC panels).
Expected outcomes: A reproducible molecular taxonomy (refining/expanding POLE/MSI/CNL/CNH paradigms), subtype‑specific pathway maps (e.g., hormone signaling, DNA repair deficiency, immune inflamed vs immune excluded), and a minimal biomarker set (RNA/IHC) for clinical assignment.

Aim 2 — Correlate subtypes with prognosis and treatment response to identify actionable therapeutic hypotheses.
Hypothesis: Molecular subtypes will stratify progression risk and predict response to standard and targeted therapies (e.g., MMRd/MSI to immunotherapy; TP53‑high CNH to anti‑HER2/PI3K strategies).
Approach: Link subtype membership to outcomes (recurrence‑free and overall survival) using multivariable Cox models adjusting for stage/grade/age. For patients with treatment annotations (adjuvant chemo, radiation, hormonal therapy, immunotherapy), estimate subtype‑specific response rates and benefit (interaction tests). Use pooled retrospective data and collaborate with institutional cohorts and clinical trial biobanks to maximize treated cases (target n≥200 with therapy annotations). Generate predictive models combining subtype + clinical covariates; assess calibration and discrimination (C‑index).
Expected outcomes: Clear prognostic stratification (low, intermediate, high risk) and identification of subtypes most likely to benefit from specific therapies, producing testable clinical hypotheses for precision trials.

Aim 3 — Functionally characterize subtype‑defining alterations and generate subtype‑matched preclinical models to assess therapeutic vulnerabilities.
Hypothesis: Key subtype drivers create distinct dependencies that can be targeted (e.g., DNA repair defects → PARP synthetic lethality; metabolic rewiring → specific metabolic inhibitors).
Approach: Prioritize 3 subtype‑specific candidate drivers from Aim 1 (e.g., recurrent kinase fusions, homologous recombination defects, immune‑modulatory alterations). Use CRISPR/overexpression in EC cell lines and patient‑derived organoids (PDOs) to model drivers and measure proliferation, invasion, and drug sensitivity (panel: PARP inhibitors, PI3K/AKT/mTOR inhibitors, CDK4/6 inhibitors, immune co‑culture assays). Establish organoid/PDX models for at least two subtypes (n≥6 models). Perform focused small‑molecule and synthetic lethal screens in subtype‑matched models.
Expected outcomes: Mechanistic validation of subtype drivers, identification of candidate targeted therapies (with in‑vitro and in‑vivo efficacy signals), and biomarker correlates (RNA/protein) to select patients for biomarker‑driven trials.

Innovation
- Multi‑omic integrative subtyping in a sufficiently powered, clinically annotated EC cohort going beyond TCGA by explicitly linking subtype to therapeutic response.
- Rapid translational loop: discovery → IHC/RNA minimal classifier → functional validation in PDO/PDX to accelerate biomarker‑driven trial design.
- Emphasis on equity: inclusion of underrepresented histologies and ancestries to ensure subtype generalizability.

Approach feasibility and milestones
- Cohort access: Institutional biobanks and TCGA + external collaborations provide sample numbers and clinical annotations.
- Technology/platforms: Established pipelines for sequencing, methylation, proteomics, and spatial assays; bioinformatics team experienced with integrative methods.
Milestones (years 1–3): Aim1 subtype publication & validated classifier (Y1–Y2); Aim2 prognostic/retrospective treatment analyses and risk model (Y2); Aim3 organoid/PDX cohort established and top drug hits identified (Y3). Go/no‑go: proceed to Aim3 once Aim1 yields ≥3 robust subtypes with classifier AUC≥0.85 and significant outcome separation (p<0.01).

Significance and long‑term impact
This program will deliver a clinically actionable molecular taxonomy of endometrial cancer, validated prognostic and predictive biomarkers, and preclinical evidence to prioritize subtype‑specific therapeutic strategies. The outputs aim to (a) improve risk stratification for adjuvant therapy decisions, (b) match patients to targeted or immunotherapies, and (c) guide prospective, biomarker‑driven clinical trials to ultimately reduce recurrence and mortality in EC.

